ng about  really is very much the opposite the  idea of euthanasia in the interests of  the individual and the case of voluntary  euthanasia the person who respecting the  wishes of the person who wants to die  otherwise of the parents or they need to  make a decision for example for a  newborn with a severe disability so I  think that these are important  distinctions and that what we need to do  is to guard against if you like the  totalitarian mentality of not thinking  that individuals are important rather  than trying to whole lines which i think  of already because of modern medical  technology become very thin very  artificial lines between what we may and  may not do when we wield so much medical  technology that we are inevitably making  life-and-death decisions  I can just follow up on that a little  bit advocates of animal rights are often  criticized that they're more concerned  with the suffering of animals and they  are with the suffering of humans how do  you reconcile say being concerned with  the animals on factory farms does that  trivialize or somehow slights a victims  of the Holocaust firstly I think the  accusation is completely false I don't  think that people in the animal rights  movement are more concerned about  animals than about humans I think  they're concerned about both and rightly  so and indeed if you look at the history  of humane reform movements you often  find a strong overlap between the two so  that it's not a coincidence I think that  it was the leaders of the anti-slavery  society who helped to stop the British  slave trade who were also many of them  among the founders of what later became  the Royal Society for the Prevention of  Cruelty to Animals and you find the same  sort of thing happening in the United  States so I think it's often a general  feeling of compassion of desire to stop  unnecessary suffering in terms of you  know is that a trivializing of the  Holocaust to be concerned about animals  in factory farms no 